{#
  How the machines of a k3s scenario become one cluster and receive the images their pods run. Each machine is labelled
  as it joins rather than afterwards, so the manifests' node selectors have something to match the moment the node
  exists.
#}
{% set server = section.machines | first %}
### Run Across Machines With k3s

{% include "readme/loopback.md.j2" %}
The machines have to form one k3s cluster first, each labelled with the machine name the manifests
pin pods to. With `k3s` installed on every machine, start the server on `{{ server.name }}`. It stays in
the foreground, so read the token the others join with from a second shell once it is up:

```bash
sudo k3s server{{ server.labels | flags("--node-label") }}
```

```bash
sudo cat /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token
```

Then join each other machine, with that token in `token`:

```bash
server={{ server.address }}
{% for machine in section.machines[1:] %}
sudo k3s agent --server "https://${server}:6443" --token "$token"{{ machine.labels | flags("--node-label") }}
{% endfor %}
```

Each image goes to the machines that run it rather than to one node, so the import steps above
become these, with each machine reachable over `ssh` by its address:

```bash
image="et-ws-server-{{ name }}:latest"
{% for address in section.run.hub_imports %}
docker save "$image" | ssh {{ address }} sudo k3s ctr images import -
{% endfor %}
{% for shipped in section.run.image_imports %}
docker save {{ shipped.image }} | ssh {{ shipped.address }} sudo k3s ctr images import -
{% endfor %}
```
